It's been many years since I read this code
carefully enough to understand it (it was fun,
though), but if I remember correctly, a newly added
room will get connected not to some _nearby_
existing room, but just to _some_ existing room,
which may well be far away behind two or three other
rooms. The corridor algorithm then tries to connect
to a doorway in the target room with a straight or
more usually an "L" shaped corridor, but when the
corridor generation runs into an intervening room,
it goes around that room's walls on one side or the
other until it can again move toward the target
room.

That's why you see so many corridors running
alongside rooms they don't enter.

There are special code cases for both shops and
vaults which I don't remember except that they do
exist.

 > Funny I didn't notice this before.

It's more subtle the smaller the map is.

